erefore, the "Triple with Single" play is the way to go here. I\'ll stick to my plan of 444+3. The end goal is to efficiently get rid of all the cards, and the plan takes care of it.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The hand contains three triples (444, QQQ, 222) and several pairs (55, 66, 99), along with scattered singles (3, 7, 8, K, A). The most efficient strategy is to play "Triples with Singles". You have exactly 3 triples and 3 weak singles (3, 7, 8). By pairing these up, you can systematically clear your hand. Playing a straight (3-4-5-6-7) would break up your triples and pairs, leaving a messy hand with too many isolated cards.\n\nReason: Starting with 444 + 3 clears the lowest triple and the lowest single immediately. This sets up a strong follow-through where you can later play QQQ + 7 and use the powerful 222 + 8 to regain control or clear the board. This leaves you with a solid structure of pairs (55, 66, 99) and high cards (K, A), including a potential pair straight (55-66).\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♦3", "♠4", "♥4", "♦4"]}'}
